    {
      "skill_id": "fr-a1-014",
      "title": "Partitive articles for food and uncounted things",
      "explanation": "To talk about some of a food, a drink or another uncounted thing, French uses a partitive article that agrees with the noun: du (masculine), de la (feminine), de l' (before a vowel or a silent h) and des (plural). Je mange du pain; Elle prend de la soupe; Nous mangeons des légumes. English often leaves this word out, but French cannot. After a negative the partitive usually shrinks to de or d': Je ne mange pas de pain.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "fr": "Il mange du poisson.",
          "en": "He is eating fish."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Elle mange de la confiture.",
          "en": "She is eating jam."
        },
        {
          "fr": "Ils mangent des fruits.",
          "en": "They are eating fruit."
        }
      ],
      "references": ["FEI-INV"],
